            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Causes of the Second Intifada</strong><br>\nThe Second Intifada began in the year 2000 and was an expression of Palestinian resistance against the Israeli occupation. The reasons for the escalation were varied, including ongoing occupation, settlement expansion, and disappointment over the lack of a peace process. Ariel Sharon's visit to the Temple Mount, a place of high religious significance, also fueled tensions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Outbreak of Violence</strong><br>\nWith the outbreak of the Second Intifada came a wave of violence. There were attacks, shootings, and clashes between Israelis and Palestinians. Both sides suffered heavy losses. The violence spread quickly, leading to numerous deaths and injuries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>International Reactions</strong><br>\nThe international community reacted with concern to the escalation of violence. Many countries and organizations called for an end to the violence and a resumption of the peace process. The United Nations, the European Union, and other international actors tried to mediate between the parties and find a solution.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Construction of the Wall</strong><br>\nIn response to the ongoing violence, Israel began building a separation wall. The aim was to increase security and prevent attacks. The wall separated many Palestinians from their workplaces, schools, and families. Construction began in 2002 and drew international criticism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Humanitarian Impacts</strong><br>\nThe wall had serious humanitarian impacts on the Palestinian population. Access to basic services was hindered, and many people suffered from the economic and social consequences. Living conditions worsened for many Palestinians, and the wall fostered mutual alienation between Israelis and Palestinians.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Impacts</strong><br>\nThe political landscape was permanently altered by the Intifada and the construction of the wall. Hopes for a two-state solution were weakened, and political leaders on both sides became more radical. Tensions between the communities intensified, and the peace process stalled.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
